<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="reading-question-non-commutativity-of-composition" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Non-commutativity of composition
  </title>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Give examples of maps <m> f </m> and <m> g </m> so that the composition <m> g \circ f </m> is well-defined but the composition <m> f \circ g </m> is not.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m> X = \set{ 0 } </m>, <m> Y = \set{ 1 } </m>, and <m> Z = \set{ 2 } </m>, and consider the maps <m> f \colon X \to Y </m> and <m> g \colon Y \to Z </m> defined by <m> f \of{ 0 } = 1 </m> and <m> g \of{ 1 } = 2 </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Since the codomain <m> Y </m> of <m> f </m> is the domain of <m> g </m>, the composition <m> g \circ f </m> is well-defined. However, since the codomain <m> Z </m> of <m> g </m> is not the domain <m> X </m> of <m> f </m>, the composition <m> f \circ g </m> is not well-defined.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Give examples of maps <m> f </m> and <m> g </m> so that the compositions <m> g \circ f </m> and <m> f \circ g </m> are well-defined but <m> g \circ f \neq f \circ g </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m> X = \set{ 0 , 1 , 2 } </m>, and consider the maps <m> f , g \colon X \to X </m> from <m> X </m> to itself defined by
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            f \of{ 0 } \amp = 1 \amp f \of{ 1 } \amp = 2 \amp f \of{ 2 } \amp = 0
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            g \of{ 0 } \amp = 1 \amp g \of{ 1 } \amp = 0 \amp g \of{ 2 } \amp = 2
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        Then
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \of{ g \circ f } \of{ 0 } \amp = g \of{ f \of{ 0 } } = g \of{ 1 } = 0
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \neq 2 = f \of{ 1 } = f \of{ g \of{ 0 } } = \of{ f \circ g } \of{ 0 }
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        and so <m> g \circ f \neq f \circ g </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</exercise>
